multiply_by_object_size uses the parameter it was given.

    void multiply_by_object_size(int object_size)
    {
        if(object_size == 1) return;                          /* tested here */
        emit_mul_register_zero_with_immediate(
            current_target->type->size, "pointer arithmetic"); /* not used here */
    }

It took the size as a parameter, TESTED that parameter, and then emitted a
different number -- re-reading the global that the caller had already changed:

    /* We need this before it's changed by the following expression  */
    int object_size = current_target->type->size;
    common_recursion(expression);
    current_target = promote_type(current_target, last_type);   <-- changed
    ...
    multiply_by_object_size(object_size);

The caller's own comment names the hazard. The value was captured to dodge it,
and then the capture was ignored.

WHY IT HID FOR SO LONG. For `struct X *p; p -= 2;` the promoted type still
happened to yield the struct's size, so the obvious test passes and the
emission looks right. It only diverges when a TYPEDEF stands between the
pointer and the struct, where the promoted type yields 8 -- the pointer's own
width -- and the pointer moves by two WORDS instead of two elements:

    struct Raw* rp;  rp -= 2;   ->   mov_x14,24     correct
    Tdef*       tp;  tp -= 2;   ->   mov_x14,8      wrong

Plain `tp = tp - 2` is fine; that path goes through pointer_element_size and
never touches this function. Only the COMPOUND forms reach it.

WHAT IT COST. tcc's value stack is exactly that shape:

    ST_DATA SValue *vtop;
    arm64_gen_opil:   vtop -= 2;  ...  ++vtop;      /* net -1, the pop */

so the pop after a binary operator moved ONE entry instead of two, and tcc's
own self-check caught it:

    int m(void){ int a; int b; return a+b; }
    error: internal compiler error: vstack leak (1)

Every binary operator with two VARIABLE operands -- + - * & < -- and every
type. `a+1` and `1+2` escaped because they constant-fold and never reach the
two-operand path, which is why the fault needed two locals to reproduce.

That diagnostic is legible at all only because EXPERIMENT-zzl gave tcc a
working stderr; before it, this was a silent wrong answer.

WHAT IT UNBLOCKS

    int m(void){ int a; int b; return a+b; }         exit 0, 1038-byte object
    int add(int,int); int main(void)                 exit 0, 1321-byte object

Multi-function translation units now compile and link into valid aarch64
objects. tcctest.c still faults and is the next frontier.

ALSO IN THIS CHANGE: two cases pinning aarch64 PADDING, which had been fixed
twice (zzh, zzi) without a broad regression test. 28 probes over alignment
invariants and over the emitted data for initialised globals -- including the
`{char*, unsigned short, unsigned short}` option-table shape that broke before
zzi. Written as INVARIANTS rather than absolute offsets, because micro-c's
eight-byte int makes absolute layouts legitimately differ from gcc's and a
test that hard-codes gcc's numbers would be measuring the wrong thing.

MEASURED

    difftest      69 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures

